nodecode-aside is a rewrite of leonphull's leonphull/hermes-aside-browser:

  https://github.com/leonphull/hermes-aside-browser
  commit ffd4e0e0de4b3a8624f3d83f98c378b88d6f9581
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Leon Phull (LICENSE)

Rewritten: the whole of the wrapper around the local `aside` CLI — the
executable lookup (the configured path, then ASIDE_BIN, then PATH, then the
places the app installs one, never a download), the five verbs (aside_status,
aside_exec, aside_repl, aside_session, aside_memory) with their argument
vectors built as a fixed list and run without a shell, the profile policy
(model, host, account, effort, speed and permission) applied to every call
while a host, an account or a permission a model sends is stripped before the
profile's own value is merged in, the refusals that fail closed when the
policy is unusable, the session actions that are list, stop, archive and
delete only because resume, steer and queue drive a session whose permission
and host the wrapper cannot verify, archive and delete requiring an explicit
confirmation, the readiness probe behind one marker, the base64 line redaction
and the 48k cut on an answer, and the pre-tool-call gate that keeps the
built-in browser out of the way while Aside answers. The skill's prose lives
in the tool descriptions and in (help :aside); the six `hermes aside ...`
commands became one section of the config and one /aside command.

Left out, and why: the `hermes aside models` catalog reader, which parsed the
Aside settings file to list model names, and with it the resolver that took a
catalog choice and turned it into a pin — a cell that pins one model in its
own section needs no model list, and reading another application's settings
directory from inside Nodecode buys nothing. Also left out: the CLI subcommand
wiring into the host's own argument parser (a cell registers /aside instead),
the MCP connector entry under catalog/ and the static mcp.json (a separate
vendor surface that this wrapper never guarded), and the Hermes-skill
installer instructions. Nothing of the original's guarantees was dropped: the
permission setting still covers aside_exec only, and the manual still says so.
